{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTCnNLsHCcay1AV8mbut","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The study is finished. Merge order clearly changes language-model quality, but it makes no detectable difference to fine-tuned classification, and most of the hypothesis's specific percentage ranges are not supported.\n\nThere were no pre-gathered papers, datasets or code, and no API key. So I built the experiment from scratch: my own BPE trainer, six tokenizers with 8k vocabularies, and 14 small (23M-parameter) GPT models, each pre-trained on the same 200 MB of Wikipedia (WikiText-103) and PubMed text. The models were scored by bits per byte (BPB) on held-out text, which is fair across tokenizers, plus 84 fine-tuning runs on SST-2, AG News and PubMed-RCT. My standard BPE trainer reproduces HuggingFace's BPE trainer exactly.\n\n| Claim | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| Standard BPE beats alternatives by 3–8% on general tasks | True for BPB (alternatives 1.8–5.8% worse, all significant); false for task accuracy (all within about 1.6%, none significant) |\n| Domain-biased merges beat standard by 5–12% on their domain | Not supported: 0.8% better domain BPB, 0.4% worse general BPB, no gain on domain classification |\n| Random order is only 2–5% worse | Roughly true for BPB (1.8–3.6%); about 0.5% and not significant on tasks |\n| Reverse-frequency degrades most, by 10–20% | Worst for BPB, but by 5.8% (general) and 9.8% (domain); not worst on tasks |\n\n- **Merge priority alone matters.** I kept the standard tokenizer's exact vocabulary and only reshuffled or reversed the order its merges are applied. That still cost 1.8–5.7% BPB, because long tokens stop being produced and 59–79% of the vocabulary goes unused.\n- **The mechanism is compression.** How many bytes each token covers perfectly predicts general-text BPB rank across the six tokenizers. The worse tokenizers lost even though they got 1.7–3.8× more training steps on the same text.\n- **Fine-tuning hides the differences.** Even the reverse-frequency tokenizer, which ends up nearly character-level, is within 1% of standard BPE on every task.\n- **Robustness:** a second random draw of each random tokenizer gave similar results, and seed-to-seed variation is far smaller than the gaps between tokenizers.\n\nThese are small models (23M parameters) with one 8k vocabulary, 2 pre-training seeds, and classification tasks with plentiful labels. Larger models, generation tasks or low-data fine-tuning could behave differently.\n\nOne host-specific problem: numpy's large allocations hung indefinitely on this machine. Setting `NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0` fixed it; it's in the README and saved to memory for future sessions.\n\nFiles are in the workspace:\n- REPORT.md\n- README.md\n- planning.md\n- src/\n- results/\n- figures/"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":423,"cache_read_input_tokens":170119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":423},"output_tokens":761,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d03f1b7b-1654-4a7a-b5d1-37b77a3f06fc","uuid":"842163d4-565c-4a1e-b513-d549b393483f","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:19:59.798Z","request_id":"req_011CgTCnMyo7Saw8LSA9NAEE"}
